### 2. I don't get how stars can be so far away. Can't we just see them up close?

**Baseline:** Stars appear close because light from them travels at a finite speed. Imagine you're on a train, and you throw a ball straight up in the air. The ball will appear to move really fast, but if you ask someone standing on the platform watching you, they'll say the ball moved really fast, but it took some time to reach them. It's the same with light from stars.

**Candidate:** Stars are huge balls of hot, glowing gas, and they're about 8 light-years away, on average. A light-year is the distance light can travel in one year, and it's about 6 trillion miles. That means if we could see stars up close, they'd be incredibly close - but they're actually so far away that light from them takes years to reach us.

